Available
---------
The package, reproduction pipeline, paper source, and static leaderboard are
public and MIT-licensed. The repository includes README, REQUIREMENTS, STATUS,
LICENSE, and INSTALL; it installs from PyPI (`pip install viyog==0.1.3`) or from
source (`uv sync --frozen`). Reproduction claims map to commands in README and
INSTALL.

Reviewed
--------
The artifact runs and produces the outputs it describes:
  - `pytest tests/` passes against the installed package.
  - `examples/quickstart.py --smoke` runs the packaged `Viyog` detector
    end-to-end on synthetic data and prints the three separation AUROCs the
    README documents.
  - `bash reproduce_t1.sh --quick` runs the *entire* T1 pipeline end to end
    on real checkpoints and real data -- reduced to one backbone, one
    attack and two OOD sets -- so a reviewer can confirm the artifact works
    on their own hardware before committing hours to the full tier. It
    preflights the machine, scales both GPU stages to the card's free VRAM,
    and verifies that every expected output file was actually produced.
  - Every script referenced in INSTALL was executed during artifact
    preparation and produces the file/output documented there.

Reproducible
------------
An independent party can regenerate the paper's core computational result
using the author-created research objects (the fine-tuned checkpoints),
methods (the packaged Viyog detector plus the experiments/ pipeline), and
conditions of analysis (the same public benchmark datasets, the same attack
library, the same signature computation) described in the paper -- not by
replaying a precomputed result.

Concretely, Tier T1 in README/INSTALL:
  1. fetches the 6 core-model checkpoints from a public HuggingFace repo
     (`amanyagami/viyog-weights` -- these are the "author-created research
     objects" the Reproducible badge definition refers to; nothing
     downstream of them is precomputed or shipped),
  2. downloads CIFAR-100 and the OOD benchmark sets via torchvision's
     standard public mirrors,
  3. regenerates adversarial examples live via `torchattacks` (FGSM, BIM,
     PGD, APGD-CE),
  4. extracts first-conv activation features with a single forward pass per
     batch,
  5. recomputes every candidate detection signature's AUROC from those
     features, including the exact statistic (`tv`, magnitude-normalised
     total variation) that ships in `src/viyog/main.py` -- the same formula,
     verified line-for-line against `experiments/06b_extract_full.py`'s
     reference extractor,
  6. and reproduces this 6-model core tier's own numbers (`ViyogD_tv_dorm`:
     T2 AUROC ~0.98, T3 AUROC ~0.84, ~0.3 KB detector state), measured
     0.9804/0.8441 on a clean-clone run of the documented commands.

     Two comparisons a reviewer should expect, both documented in README:

     (a) The committed CSVs in `results/analysis/` read 0.940/0.777 rather
         than 0.9804/0.8441. They were generated over the full SIX-attack
         suite (adding DeepFool and CW) whereas the documented reproduce
         command runs four (FGSM, BIM, PGD, APGD-CE). DeepFool and CW are
         minimal-perturbation attacks -- they stop at the decision boundary,
         leaving far less first-layer residue -- and score only T2 0.78/0.70
         on their own, which is what lowers the six-attack mean. The
         four-attack mean of the per-attack table (0.98035) matches the
         fresh run to within 0.0001. Both numbers are correct for their
         respective attack sets; README carries the full per-attack
         breakdown so the difference is checkable, not surprising.

     (b) Neither is the paper's headline 0.980/0.844,
         which averages the 17-architecture held-out panel (Tier T2 -- 20
         architectures minus MobileOne-S1, ResNet-18, and ResNet-34, whose
         archived first-layer features were found to be identically zero).
         Reproducing that exactly requires the full Tier-T2 sweep over the
         17-architecture panel.

This was executed end-to-end during artifact preparation on a single CUDA
GPU (an H200); see REQUIREMENTS for what hardware a reviewer needs and
INSTALL for the exact commands and expected outputs. Real measured
wall-clock on a genuine from-scratch fresh-clone run (git clone into an
empty directory, nothing cached anywhere): adversarial generation for all
6 models x 4 attacks = **2h 6min** (APGD-CE's 100 gradient steps/batch on
the slower architectures is the long pole); first-conv feature extraction
= **2h 0min** (dominated by first-time OOD dataset downloads over a
throttled connection, not compute); signature recomputation (5s),
`full_eval` (11s), the master comparison table (<1s) and `make demo-data`
(1s) are all effectively instant. Total **4h 7min**. **Budget 4+ hours for
an honest first run.** A second run against the same clone is far faster,
since generated files aren't regenerated and downloaded datasets aren't
re-downloaded.

These are not estimates: they are the step timings printed by the
2026-07-29 clean-clone run (7588s / 7197s / 5s / 11s / 0s / 1s;
14802s total), which completed every documented step without error.

Two earlier drafts of this file understated these numbers, both caught by
actually re-running the documented commands against a fresh clone rather
than trusting figures measured in an already-warm working directory: the
generation step at "22 seconds" (that was a re-run timing a
skip-existing-file check, not real generation), then extraction at
"~44 min" (measured in a working directory that already had some OOD
datasets cached from unrelated prior research on the same machine, not a
genuine zero-cache run).

What this artifact does not claim
-------------------------------------
- Tier T3 (re-finetuning all 20 backbones from raw ImageNet weights) is
  GPU-weeks of compute and is documented but explicitly out of scope --
  this does not weaken the Reproducible claim above, which rests on T1.
- The full 17-architecture (of the original 20; see the data-integrity note
  below), 3-ID-dataset sweep (Tier T2) is available and was run during
  development, but is offered as additional evidence, not as part of the
  required reproduction path.
- Data integrity: the archived first-layer feature files for MobileOne-S1,
  ResNet-18, and ResNet-34 were found to contain identically-zero ID tensors
  (an extraction failure). `experiments/full_eval.py` now detects and
  excludes any such file automatically, writing
  `results/analysis/full_eval_<dataset>_exclusions.csv`. All headline
  numbers in this artifact and the paper report the resulting 17-architecture
  panel; these three architectures' features were not re-extracted for this
  submission.
- One dataset variant (GTSRB) has an analysis file
  (`results/gtsrb/analysis/baselines_feature_gtsrb.csv`) that is currently
  empty on the development machine due to an unrelated data-pipeline gap;
  this affects only the GTSRB row of the extended (T2) multi-dataset
  comparison, not the required cifar100 T1 tier.

Known, disclosed limitation (fixed, not hidden)
----------------------------------------------------
During artifact preparation, a bug was found in the systems/efficiency
measurement code (`eval_systems.py`, `eval_detector_cost.py`): a hand-rolled
MAC-counting hook undercounted full-model compute on attention-based and
per-position-Linear architectures (ViT, Swin, ConvNeXtV2, EdgeNeXt) by
10-100x, because it only instrumented Conv2d/Linear layers. This was caught
via a cross-check against `fvcore`'s trace-based FlopCountAnalysis, fixed
(fvcore is now the default, the old hook is kept as an audit-trail column),
and the affected downstream data (`results/analysis/systems_cifar100.csv`,
`detector_cost_compute.csv`, `master_comparison_{cifar100,cifar10}.csv`,
and the live demo leaderboard's compute-cost column) was regenerated. The
direction of the original error was conservative -- it understated Viyog's
efficiency advantage on those four architectures, not the reverse -- and it
does not affect any detection-accuracy number (T1/T2/T3 AUROC, state
memory) reported elsewhere in the paper or this artifact.

Second disclosed limitation: in-sample dormant-band selection
-------------------------------------------------------------------
The reproduction pipeline selects the dormant channel band and the ID
reference statistics from the same ID split it then scores as the
negative class (`09_signatures_full.py`, `08_signatures.py` both load a
single `*_id.h5` -- the CIFAR-100 test split -- compute the per-channel
ranking from it, and reuse that same array as the ID population in the
reported AUROCs). There is no held-out calibration split in that path.

Three things bound what this can and cannot do to the reported numbers,
and we state them precisely rather than asserting the effect is zero:

  - The band selection is *unsupervised with respect to the decision
    being measured*. It ranks channels by mean ID activation only; it
    never sees an adversarial or OOD sample, and never sees a label.
    This is not test-label leakage -- it is unsupervised feature
    selection performed on a population that is later scored.
  - The ID-mean centering (`id_score_mean_`) is a rank-invariant shift
    and therefore cannot change AUROC at all.
  - What remains is a real but narrow optimism: the "quietest" channels
    are chosen using exactly the ID sample they are later evaluated
    against, so the ID score distribution can be marginally better
    behaved than it would be on unseen ID data.

The packaged detector does not have this gap -- `Viyog.fit(id_loader)`
then `.score()` on new data is the documented contract, and
`examples/quickstart.py` splits its ID pool into disjoint fit and score
halves. The gap is specific to the research pipeline that produced the
paper's tables.

We are disclosing rather than silently "fixing" this, deliberately. The
artifact's obligation for the Reproducible badge is to regenerate *the
numbers the paper reports*, under the protocol the paper used. Changing
the evaluation protocol now would make the artifact stop reproducing the
accepted paper -- which is a paper revision, not an artifact fix. The
held-out-split variant is the right protocol for the extended version of
this work, and a reviewer who wants to quantify the effect can do so
directly: split the ID feature array in `analyse_model()`, fit the band
on one half, score the other.
